- How would you extend to a constant-time generic rotate for arbitrary bit-widths?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of non-trivial bit hacks and understanding of 8×8 transpose, a common optimization in graphics, crypto, and compression. It checks for precise, branchless manipulation and correct use of masks.

## Key Concepts
- fixed-sequence bit swaps
- 8×8 bit matrix representation
- mask constants, shifts, no loops

## Code Example
```cpp
uint64_t transpose64(uint64_t x) {
    const uint64_t m1  = 0x5555555555555555ULL;
    x = (x & m1) << 1 | (x >> 1) & m1;
    const uint64_t m2  = 0x3333333333333333ULL;
    x = (x & m2) << 2 | (x >> 2) & m2;
    const uint64_t m4  = 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
    x = (x & m4) << 4 | (x >> 4) & m4;
    const uint64_t m8  = 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
    x = (x & m8) << 8 | (x >> 8) & m8;
    const uint64_t m16 = 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
    x = (x & m16) << 16 | (x >> 16) & m16;
    const uint64_t m32 = 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL;
    x = (x & m32) << 32 | (x >> 32) & m32;
    return x;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test this with randomized 64-bit inputs to validate correctness?
- How would you generalize to 16×16 blocks with a 256-bit input stored in four 64-bit words?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses non-branching bit extraction (compression-like utility) crucial in low-latency paths. It tests SWAR techniques, mask reasoning, and edge-case handling for arbitrary masks.\n\n## Key Concepts
- SWAR bit-twiddling for packing bits\n- Mask-driven selection and order preservation\n- Handling 64-bit boundaries without loops\n\n## Code Example
```c
uint64_t compressBits64(uint64_t x, uint64_t mask) {
  x &= mask;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 1)) & 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 2)) & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 8)) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 16)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 32)) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL;
  return x;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 128-bit inputs?\n- What are the performance implications on modern CPUs with this fixed-sequence approach?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests understanding of fixed-depth carry logic and 128-bit arithmetic without loops—critical in high-throughput code paths.

## Key Concepts

- **Carry-lookahead across 4×32-bit lanes**: Break 128 bits into four 32-bit chunks for parallel carry computation.
- **Propagate (P) and Generate (G) signals**: P = a ^ b (carry passes through), G = a & b (carry generated).
- **Fixed-depth carry computation**: Uses only OR/AND operations, achieving O(1) depth.
- **Correct 128-bit carry propagation**: Ensure carries propagate correctly across all four lanes.

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 add128(uint128 a, uint128 b) {
    uint64_t a0 = (uint32_t)a.lo;
    uint64_t a1 = (uint32_t)(a.lo >> 32);
    uint64_t a2 = (uint32_t)a.hi;
    uint64_t a3 = (uint32_t)(a.hi >> 32);

    uint64_t b0 = (uint32_t)b.lo;
    uint64_t b1 = (uint32_t)(b.lo >> 32);
    uint64_t b2 = (uint32_t)b.hi;
    uint64_t b3 = (uint32_t)(b.hi >> 32);

    uint64_t p0 = a0 ^ b0, g0 = a0 & b0;
    uint64_t p1 = a1 ^ b1, g1 = a1 & b1;
    uint64_t p2 = a2 ^ b2, g2 = a2 & b2;
    uint64_t p3 = a3 ^ b3, g3 = a3 & b3;

    uint64_t c1 = g0;
    uint64_t c2 = g0 | (p0 & g1);
    uint64_t c3 = g0 | (p0 & g1) | (p0 & p1 & g2);
    uint64_t c4 = g0 | (p0 & g1) | (p0 & p1 & g2) | (p0 & p1 & p2 & g3);

    uint128 r;
    r.lo = (p3 ^ c4) | ((p2 ^ c3) << 32) | ((p1 ^ c2) << 64) | ((p0 ^ c1) << 96);
    r.hi = (p3 ^ c4) >> 32;
    return r;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This asks for deep bit-manipulation skill: a palindrome across 128 bits is a nontrivial symmetry that can be checked in constant time with a reversal network, not with loops. It reveals understanding of endian-ness and 128-bit layout.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit word as {uint64 hi, lo}
- Bit-reversal networks using masks: 0x5555..., 0x3333..., 0x0F0F..., etc.
- Palindrome property across all 128 bits
- Edge-case tests (all zeros, ends-set pattern)

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t x){
  x = (x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL | (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) << 1;
  x = (x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL | (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) << 2;
  x = (x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL | (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) << 4;
  x = (x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL | (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8;
  x = (x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL | (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16;
  x = (x >> 32) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL | (x & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL) << 32;
  return x;
}
static inline uint128 reverse128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r; r.lo = reverse64(x.hi); r.hi = reverse64(x.lo); return r;
}
int isPalindromicBits128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r = reverse128(x);
  return (x.hi == r.hi) && (x.lo == r.lo);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to support streaming 128-bit palindromicity with partial data?
- What are the performance implications on 64-bit vs 128-bit targets?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to implement a hardware-style popcount without loops or built-in functions, demonstrating understanding of bitwise manipulation techniques.

## Key Concepts
- Parallel bit counting using bitmasks
- 32-bit unsigned arithmetic with logical shifts
- Elimination of branches and loops for performance

## Code Example
```javascript
function countBits32(n) {
  n = n - ((n >>> 1) & 0x55555555);
  n = (n & 0x33333333) + ((n >>> 2) & 0x33333333);
  n = (n + (n >>> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  n = n + (n >>> 8);
  n = n + (n >>> 16);
  return n & 0x3F;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would this differ for a 64-bit implementation?
- Can you optimize this for specific input patterns?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of branchless, scalable bit-twiddling techniques for 64-bit inputs.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR style bit reversal
- Masking and shifting sequences
- Branchless algorithms for performance

## Code Example
```c
#include <stdint.h>

uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t n) {
  n = ((n >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL) | ((n & 0x5555555555555555ULL) << 1);
  n = ((n >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL) | ((n & 0x3333333333333333ULL) << 2);
  n = ((n >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) | ((n & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) << 4);
  n = ((n >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) | ((n & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8);
  n = ((n >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) | ((n & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16);
  n = (n >> 32) | (n << 32);
  return n;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize for smaller word sizes or specialized ISAs?
- How would you adapt for 128-bit inputs and performance considerations?

## Why This Is Asked
Interleaving bits to form Morton codes is common in spatial indexing and GPU data layouts. The solution enforces branchless bit spreading using fixed masks, ensuring deterministic performance.

## Key Concepts
- Morton order (Z-order) bit interleaving
- Fixed-mask bit spreading (part1by1)
- Branchless implementation

## Code Example
```javascript
static inline uint64_t part1by1(uint32_t n) {
  uint64_t v = n;
  v = (v | (v << 16)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
  v = (v | (v << 8))  & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
  v = (v | (v << 4))  & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  v = (v | (v << 2))  & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
  v = (v | (v << 1))  & 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  return v;
}

uint64_t interleave32(uint32_t x, uint32_t y) {
  return (part1by1(y) << 1) | part1by1(x);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 64-bit inputs producing a 128-bit result?
- How does this interact with SIMD paths for batched data?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests per-byte parallel parity computation with pure bitwise ops.\n\n## Key Concepts
- 128-bit lane handling via two 64-bit halves\n- In-place parity reduction using shifts\n- Bitwise masking to extract per-byte results\n\n## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
uint128 perByteParity128(uint128 x){
  uint64_t lo = x.lo, hi = x.hi;
  lo ^= lo >> 4; lo ^= lo >> 2; lo ^= lo >> 1;
  hi ^= hi >> 4; hi ^= hi >> 2; hi ^= hi >> 1;
  uint64_t mask = 0x0101010101010101ULL;
  return (uint128){ .lo = lo & mask, .hi = hi & mask };
}
```
## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for non-byte-aligned lanes?\n- How would you verify correctness with minimal tests?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to perform full-width bit reversal in constant time using SWAR patterns, not per-bit loops.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit composition as hi/lo 64-bit
- SWAR bit-reversal: 1,2,4,8,16,32-bit block swaps
- Final cross-half swap to complete reversal

## Code Example
```javascript
// 64-bit reversal helper
static uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t x){
  x = (x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL | (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) << 1;
  x = (x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL | (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) << 2;
  x = (x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL | (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) << 4;
  x = (x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL | (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8;
  x = (x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL | (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16;
  x = (x >> 32) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL | (x & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL) << 32;
  return x;
}
uint128 reverse128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r;
  r.lo = reverse64(x.hi);
  r.hi = reverse64(x.lo);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to larger widths (256-bit) with minimal extra code?
- What intrinsics or compiler options help optimize this on typical CPUs?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to perform cross-128-bit bit counting without loops, using portable bit-twiddling. Demonstrates knowledge of XOR for difference, and multi-word popcount without hardware intrinsics.

## Key Concepts

- XOR-based difference
- SWAR-style popcount on 64-bit words
- Combining counts for 128-bit data

## Code Example

```c
unsigned distance128(uint128 a, uint128 b) {
  uint64_t x = a.hi ^ b.hi;
  uint64_t y = a.lo ^ b.lo;
  // SWAR popcount on x
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  x = x + (x >> 8);
  x = x + (x >> 16);
  x = x + (x >> 32);
  // SWAR popcount on y
  y = y - ((y >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  y = (y & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((y >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  y = (y + (y >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  y = y + (y >> 8);
  y = y + (y >> 16);
  y = y + (y >> 32);
  return (unsigned)(x & 0xFF) + (unsigned)(y & 0xFF);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you generalize to arbitrary n-word widths while maintaining no-loops constraints?
- Could you optimize using compiler intrinsics if available and fall back gracefully? If so, which intrinsics and how would you keep portability?
